From 9dd67ad027aa115ea945e25884f2558d9e4de8c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: iasnezhkov <iasnezhkov@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 19:40:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: record-once/replay-per-frame MDP fast path for
 MdpImageConverter

Adds mdpreplay::MdpReplayEngine (v4l2/MdpReplay.{h,cpp}, exported header
v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/MdpReplay.h) and wires it into
MdpImageConverter::convertMM21ToNV12 (patch 6) so a stream's MM21->NV12
DpBlitStream command buffer is captured once per (width, height, src/dst
plane size) shape and replayed directly against /dev/mtk_mdp for every
later frame of that shape, only patching the WRITE_FD/WRITE_FD_RDMA src
and dst fd fields, instead of paying DpBlitStream's per-frame C++/JNI-less
but still non-trivial call cost every time.

This is the same technique tools/rigs/mdpreplay.c already proved on the
device this session: a real decoder frame (640x480 MM21) replayed through
an fd-substituted capture matched DpBlitStream's own output byte for byte
("phase B frame 0 PASS", logs/o66_1-2309/mdp-fast.txt), and replay cost on
kernel G5 was measured at 1920x1088 wall 7.45 ms/frame (sys 3.21 ms, user
0.003 ms), 640x480 wall 1.96 ms (sys 1.20 ms), against DpBlitStream's own
11.5-14 ms/frame with real user CPU. This patch does not re-measure any of
that; it productionizes the same struct layouts and capture/replay
algorithm as a per-instance engine instead of mdpreplay.c's single-shot,
whole-process globals, so two decoder instances can each run their own
capture without sharing state, and ships an NDK test driver
(tools/rigs/mdpreplayhal-test.cpp) that reproduces mdpreplay.c's phase A/B
checks through this engine's own API, not a second implementation of the
technique.

Struct reuse: OpMeta/MdpSubmit/MdpWait/CmdqSecDataStruct/MdpPmqos and the
CMDQ_IOCTL_* request numbers in MdpReplay.h are the same layout as
tools/rigs/mdpreplay.c's op_meta/mdp_submit/mdp_wait/cmdqSecDataStruct,
transcribed field-for-field (not derived from a kernel header, since none
is exported to userspace here) and re-asserted with the same
sizeof()==336/16/48/264 static_asserts mdpreplay.c already carries. The
_IOW/_IOR request numbers are computed at compile time from the same
magic/nr/size triples mdpreplay.c uses, spelled out from
<asm-generic/ioctl.h>'s bit layout instead of including <sys/ioctl.h>, so
MdpReplay.h has zero system-header dependencies beyond <cstdint>/
<cstddef> and compiles unmodified in both the HAL tree and a plain NDK
executable. mdpreplay.c itself is treated as read-only reference for this
work (main thread commit 567ec41 added a -D option to it); none of its
code is reused verbatim, only the struct layout and algorithm it already
proved.

Byte-identity between tools/rigs/ and this patch: the copies this patch
adds at v4l2/MdpReplay.cpp, v4l2/include/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/MdpReplay.h and
service/MdpReplayInterpose.cpp were produced by `diff -q` against
tools/rigs/MdpReplay.cpp, tools/rigs/v4l2_codec2/v4l2/MdpReplay.h and
tools/rigs/MdpReplayInterpose.cpp respectively before this patch was
generated (all three reported identical); the NDK test driver
(tools/rigs/mdpreplayhal-test.cpp) compiles those same three tools/rigs/
files directly, unmodified, so the engine this patch ships is exactly the
engine the test driver exercises, not a fork of it. Re-checking this after
any future edit to either copy is one `diff` invocation per file; nothing
generates one copy from the other automatically, so a straight text edit
to only one side will silently break the claim until someone runs that
diff again.

Interposition mechanism (deliverable: ioctl() defined in the service
executable, not a shared lib): the service binary for this v4l2-based
Codec2 HAL is android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2
(service/Android.bp, service/service.cpp, fetched from
https://android.googlesource.com/platform/external/v4l2_codec2
refs/heads/main on 23.09.2026 since this task is device-free and cannot
read the checked-out tree in the Lima guest). service/MdpReplayInterpose.cpp
adds a process-wide `extern "C" int ioctl(int, int, ...)` there, forwarding
to mdpreplay::ActiveRecordingEngine() (a thread_local getter defined in
MdpReplay.cpp, exported from libv4l2_codec2_v4l2.so, which the service
binary already links via shared_libs per the real Android.bp above) and
otherwise falling through to `syscall(__NR_ioctl, ...)`, exactly
mdpreplay.c's own interposition technique and its own documented
rationale for why it works from the root executable without LD_PRELOAD:
"the executable is always part of the global symbol group". This patch
adds `ldflags: ["-Wl,--export-dynamic"]` (the standard Soong spelling of
-rdynamic for a cc_binary, confirmed against a real Android.bp on this
machine: platform/frameworks/base's app_process, which carries the
identical "-Wl,--export-dynamic" ldflags entry for the same reason -
exporting its own symbols to libraries it does not dlopen itself) to the
android.hardware.media.c2@1.2-service-v4l2 cc_binary so libdpframework.so
resolves `ioctl` against this symbol instead of libc's, process-wide, the
same way it already does inside tools/rigs/mdpreplay.c on the device.
MdpImageConverter.cpp itself never calls ioctl() and does not need to
link anything new: it only calls MdpReplayEngine::BeginRecord/EndRecord/
Replay, which live in MdpReplay.cpp (v4l2/Android.bp already builds that
file into libv4l2_codec2_v4l2.so once added to srcs, below).

Thread safety for two concurrent decoder instances (each owning its own
MdpImageConverter and hence its own MdpReplayEngine): the "active
recording engine" is a thread_local pointer, set by BeginRecord() and
cleared by EndRecord(), bracketing exactly the DpBlitStream::invalidate()
call on whichever thread called convertMM21ToNV12() - normally each
V4L2Decoder's own device thread. Two decoders' device threads therefore
never see each other's capture even though the interposed ioctl() symbol
is one process-wide C function, because thread_local storage is
per-thread by construction; no additional lock is added or needed for
this, matching the task's own suggested design.

MdpImageConverter::convertMM21ToNV12 change: computes an mdpreplay::Config
key (width, height, and the same srcPlaneSizes/dstPlaneSizes values
already being computed for setSrcBuffer/setDstBuffer, unchanged) before
doing anything else; if mEngine already holds a ready capture for that
exact Config, it tries mEngine.Replay(src.dmaBufFd, dst.dmaBufFd) and
returns immediately on success. Otherwise (first frame of a stream, a
resolution change, or Replay() having failed a call it did not disarm -
see below) it falls through to exactly the same
setSrcConfig/setSrcBuffer/setDstConfig/setDstBuffer/invalidate sequence
patch 6 already had, now bracketed by
mEngine.BeginRecord(cfg, ...)/mEngine.EndRecord(cfg, ..., reason) around
the invalidate() call only (matching mdpreplay.c's own g_hook_active
window, since setSrcConfig/setSrcBuffer/etc. do not themselves reach
CMDQ_IOCTL_ASYNC_EXEC). EndRecord's own validation refuses the capture
(logged via ALOGI, non-fatal) on: invalidate() itself failing, the
interposed ioctl() never firing at all (the same self-verification
mdpreplay.c does, catching a linker-namespace miss instead of silently
reporting nothing), any WRITE_FD/WRITE_FD_RDMA op_meta whose fd is
neither the record-time src nor dst fd (no unknown buffers, per the task
brief), or a job with meta_count above the 512-entry cap (no truncation).
The frame in flight always completes via DpBlitStream's own return value
in this branch, unchanged from patch 6 - MdpReplayEngine only ever adds a
faster path for later frames, never blocks the current one. Fallback
chain end to end: MdpReplayEngine::Replay() -> DpBlitStream (both inside
MdpImageConverter) -> LibyuvImageConverter (VideoFrame::detileFrom's
existing primaryFailed plumbing from patch 6, untouched by this patch).

Open hypothesis, not exercised on a device: if Replay() fails on a config
that still matches (e.g. a transient hardware/queue error, not a
resolution change), MdpImageConverter falls through to the DpBlitStream
path for that one frame, which re-arms the capture; it does not back off
after repeated failures. A persistent (non-transient) replay fault would
therefore cost one extra failed CMDQ_IOCTL_ASYNC_EXEC attempt on top of
the DpBlitStream fallback on every single frame rather than disabling
itself, since there is no device data yet to calibrate a failure
threshold; flagged here rather than guessed at.

Open hypothesis, not exercised on a device: MdpReplayEngine::teardown()
calls CMDQ_IOCTL_IOVA_UNMAP (kernel commits 8209a3bb8 + 1d68754de) for
every distinct fd Replay() has ever submitted, capped at the last 16
(trackFd()'s ring), when the engine itself is destroyed. Those fds belong
to the Codec2 graphic block pool, not to this engine, and the pool may
already have closed and even reused some of those fd numbers for
unrelated buffers well before the engine is torn down. Per the kernel
patch's own ENOENT-tolerant contract (already relied on by
tools/rigs/mdpreplay.c's iova_unmap_one()), unmapping a stale or reused fd
number should at worst force one extra remap on that fd's next real use,
not corrupt anything, since IOVA_UNMAP only drops a cached mapping, never
touches buffer content directly - this reasoning is unverified on real
hardware and listed here rather than assumed correct.

AOSP/Soong surface this patch relies on, so it can be checked without a
build: Android.bp `srcs`/`shared_libs`/`ldflags` are ordinary Soong
cc_library/cc_binary properties (no `allow_undefined_symbols` or other
exotic property is needed anywhere - the executable's ioctl() reaches
mdpreplay::ActiveRecordingEngine() as an ordinary call into a shared_libs
dependency the service binary already declares, and
libv4l2_codec2_v4l2.so itself fully resolves every symbol MdpReplay.cpp
needs, libc only, at its own link time); GraphicBufferMapper/plane layout
and the DpBlitStream mangled-symbol redeclaration are unchanged from
patch 6 and not touched here.

